  "summary": "Mario Party 8 is Hudson Soft's Wii debut for the party series, released in North America on 29 May 2007.",
  "body": [
    "Mario Party 8 is Hudson Soft's Wii debut for the party series, released in North America on 29 May 2007. MC Ballyhoo and his talking hat host a carnival of boards and minigames. Four competitors collect Stars while movement, items and events change their fortunes between the short challenges. The Wii Remote gives the gathering another physical vocabulary, making a minigame's instruction something the player may answer with a swing, a point or a tilt as well as a conventional button press.",
    "The six boards provide different settings for the race, while the Party Tent offers free-for-all, paired and one-on-one arrangements. The player still needs to consider the next Star and the items that can help reach it, but the minigames change how success is demonstrated. Moving between ordinary board turns and gesture-based tasks lets the controller support both a familiar strategic rhythm and an immediately visible action. The group can watch another person's movement in the room as well as the character's response on screen.",
    "Separate areas collect minigame challenges, extra activities and unlockable rewards, with some games allowing the use of Miis. Star Battle Arena provides a one-on-one progression across the boards for a solo player. Carnival cards purchase further content, including music and character voices, while Yoshihiro Tsukahara's score supports the festive setting. Mario Party 8 carries the series into Wii's broader social approach to control, keeping its board-game foundation while making the next short challenge an opportunity to pass around a different kind of controller."
  ],
  "story_headings": [
    "The carnival reaches the Wii Remote",
    "A small gesture becomes the next challenge",
    "A familiar board with a different controller"
  ],
